# ---------------- M33 expansions ----------------
EXP[('IL29-M33',0)] = """One more exam-day mechanic: CompTIA randomizes question order, so your first pass is really a hunt for quick wins across the whole exam. Practice this on every timed set - never answer sequentially out of habit. And remember that PBQs are often front-loaded; if the first screen is a simulation, breathe, flag it, and start with question two. The points are identical wherever they sit, and confidence compounds: banking ten quick wins in the first fifteen minutes changes how you handle the hard questions later."""
EXP[('IL29-M33',1)] = """A third worked example ties it together. Scenario: 'A company stores backups offline in a fireproof safe and tests restores quarterly.' Classification: the offline backup is a corrective control (it restores operations after an incident), implemented technically and physically, and the quarterly test is a managerial detective control (it verifies the corrective control works). Exam stems often bundle two controls - classify each separately and you will not be fooled. Also drill the zero-trust vocabulary until it is boring: 'never trust, always verify' is the phrase the exam wants, and every scenario about implicit trust (VPN users treated as safe, internal traffic uninspected) is a zero-trust violation. When a question asks for the BEST long-term fix for lateral movement after a phishing breach, the zero-trust answer - microsegmentation plus least privilege - beats 'a bigger firewall' every time."""
EXP[('IL29-M33',2)] = """A third worked example - attack surface reasoning. Scenario: 'An attacker scans the public IP range, finds an exposed RDP port, and brute-forces a weak password.' The attack vector is the exposed RDP service (attack surface: internet-facing remote access); the vulnerability is the weak password plus the exposed service; the mitigation stack is layered - remove RDP from the internet (attack-surface reduction), enforce MFA and account lockout (credential hardening), and put the service behind a VPN or jump host (network control). Exam questions love asking for the BEST mitigation, and the best answer is usually the one that shrinks the attack surface rather than the one that polices it. Common trap: choosing 'install antivirus' for a network-exposure problem - the vector is the exposure, so the fix must address the exposure. Another: confusing a vulnerability (the weakness) with a threat (the actor or event that exploits it). The weak password is the vulnerability; the brute-forcing attacker is the threat; together they create risk. Keep those three nouns straight and Domain 2.0 scenario stems become classification exercises."""
EXP[('IL29-M33',3)] = """A third worked example - resilience math in plain language. 'The business needs the ordering system back within 4 hours of a failure, and can afford to lose at most 1 hour of transactions.' That is an RTO of 4 hours (recovery time objective: how fast) and an RPO of 1 hour (recovery point objective: how much data loss). The architecture answer: backups at least hourly (to meet the RPO) plus a tested restore procedure that completes inside 4 hours (to meet the RTO). Exam stems give you the business requirement and ask which backup strategy fits - match the frequency to the RPO and the restore speed to the RTO. Common mistake: swapping the two. RTO is about time to recover; RPO is about data loss tolerance. A second gotcha: high availability (keeping the system running through failures - clustering, redundancy) vs. fault tolerance (continuing through a component failure with zero interruption). Both serve availability, but fault tolerance is the stronger guarantee."""
EXP[('IL29-M33',4)] = """A third worked example - the EDR vs. antivirus question the exam loves. 'A fileless attack runs entirely in memory via PowerShell.' Traditional signature antivirus sees no file and stays silent; EDR sees the process behavior - PowerShell spawning from Word, obfuscated commands, C2 beaconing - and kills the chain. The exam discriminator: signature-based tools match known files; behavior-based tools (EDR) match malicious actions. When the stem says 'fileless,' 'living off the land,' or 'zero-day,' the answer is the behavioral tool. Common mistake: treating DLP as a network-only control - DLP spans endpoint, network, and cloud, watching data in use, in motion, and at rest. And a final Domain 4.0 trap: penetration testing requires written authorization defining scope - the exam treats an unauthorized 'helpful' test as an attack, and the correct answer always starts with the rules of engagement."""
EXP[('IL29-M33',5)] = """A second worked example - the policy hierarchy. 'An employee violates the acceptable-use policy by mining crypto on a workstation.' The policy (managerial, directive) defined the rule; the monitoring that caught it was detective; the disciplinary process is the enforcement. Exam questions ask which document governs a situation - policies set direction, standards set the mandatory rules, procedures give step-by-step instructions, guidelines are recommendations. The discriminator: 'must' language lives in standards, 'how to' lives in procedures. A third mini-example: third-party risk. 'The company adopts a SaaS vendor that suffers a breach.' The controls that should have existed: a vendor risk assessment before signing, a right-to-audit clause in the contract, and a review of the vendor's SOC 2 report. On the exam, supply-chain and vendor questions are always answered with assessment-before-trust - never 'the vendor handles it.'"""
EXP[('IL29-M33',6)] = """One more planning tool: the error log. After every practice set, record each miss in four buckets - knowledge gap (never learned it), misread stem (knew it, rushed it), pacing casualty (ran out of time), distractor trap (fell for the tempting wrong answer). After three sets, the buckets reveal your real enemy: knowledge gaps need study blocks, misreads need slower first-pass discipline, pacing casualties need stricter flag-and-return, distractor traps need the 'what is the question REALLY asking' habit. Most candidates discover their enemy is not knowledge but process - and process is fixable in days, not weeks. Review the log before each new set; watching the same trap catch you twice is the only unforgivable error in this plan."""

# ---------------- M34 expansions ----------------
EXP[('IL29-M34',0)] = """A second pacing scenario, because CySA+ PBQs are the real time sink. Suppose the exam serves you 80 multiple-choice and 5 PBQs. At two minutes each, the multiple-choice costs 160 minutes - already over budget. That arithmetic is why flag-and-return is non-negotiable: answer the 60 multiple-choice you know in about 70 minutes, bank them, then spend the remaining 95 minutes on the 20 harder multiple-choice plus the 5 PBQs. The candidates who fail CySA+ on time usually did the opposite - they wrestled each PBQ in order and never reached the last 25 questions. Also note the psychological shape: CySA+ stems are longer than Security+'s, with log snippets and scenario setups. Train yourself to read the actual question FIRST (the last sentence), then read the stem for exactly what it asks. Analysts who read the whole log bundle before knowing the question burn ten minutes on data they never needed - the same discipline as a real SOC queue, and the exam is deliberately testing it."""
EXP[('IL29-M34',1)] = """A third worked example - alert tuning as an exam scenario. 'A correlation rule fires 300 times daily on legitimate vulnerability-scanner traffic, and analysts now ignore the rule entirely.' The diagnosis: a false-positive flood causing alert fatigue - which is operationally equivalent to a false negative, because real attacks hide in the ignored noise. The fix: tune the rule to exclude the scanner's IP range or require a second indicator (e.g., scanner traffic PLUS an unexpected process launch), then verify the tuned rule still fires on a known-bad test case. The exam tests this as a process question: the wrong answers are 'disable the rule' (creates a real blind spot) and 'hire more analysts' (scales the noise). The right answer is always tuning with verification. Common mistake: confusing continuous monitoring with a tool purchase. Monitoring is the practice - log sources feeding correlation feeding triage, around the clock. The exam treats 'we bought a SIEM but nobody watches the queue on weekends' as a monitoring failure, and the finding is process, not product."""
EXP[('IL29-M34',2)] = """A third worked example - the scan-types discriminator the exam loves. 'The team needs a complete inventory of missing patches on 500 workstations, including registry-level detail.' The answer is a credentialed scan: with valid credentials, the scanner reads the registry and patch history directly - accurate and fast. A non-credentialed scan would only infer from network banners, missing half the findings. Contrast: 'The team wants to see what an external attacker sees.' That is a non-credentialed, external scan - no credentials, attacker's view. The exam pairs these deliberately; match the scan type to the goal. A second trap: scan timing. Credentialed scans during business hours can disrupt operations - the analyst's job includes scheduling, change control, and stakeholder notice, which is why vulnerability management is a program, not a button. And remember the lifecycle loop: scan, validate (kill false positives before you alarm anyone), prioritize (exploitability x exposure x criticality), remediate (change-controlled), rescan (verify closure). The exam's favorite wrong answer is any option that skips validation or the rescan - unverified findings waste everyone's time, and unverified fixes are open findings."""
EXP[('IL29-M34',3)] = """A third worked example - the tabletop as preparation. 'The team runs a tabletop exercise: a simulated ransomware outbreak on a Friday afternoon.' The facilitator walks through each phase: who declares the incident, who isolates, who talks to legal, who decides on restoration. The value is not the scenario - it is discovering that nobody knows who can authorize isolating the finance VLAN. That discovery IS the exercise's product, and the playbook gets updated before a real incident tests it. The exam treats tabletops as a preparation-phase control and loves asking what a tabletop produces (findings about the plan, not about the attacker). Common mistake: confusing incident response with disaster recovery. IR handles the security event (stop the attacker, eradicate, recover securely); DR handles the restoration of IT services after any disruption (the RTO/RPO plan). They overlap at recovery but start from different triggers - the exam separates them by asking what initiated the response. A security incident triggers IR; a hurricane triggers DR and business continuity. Know which plan owns which trigger."""
EXP[('IL29-M34',4)] = """A second worked example - metrics that matter. 'The CISO asks whether the SOC is improving.' The analyst answers with three numbers: mean time to detect (MTTD) dropped from 4 hours to 45 minutes after the new correlation rules; mean time to respond (MTTR) dropped from 8 hours to 2 hours after the playbook update; and vulnerability-remediation SLA compliance rose from 60% to 92%. Those three metrics - MTTD, MTTR, SLA compliance - are the exam's favorite proof-of-program numbers, and any answer choice offering vanity metrics ('alerts processed,' 'logs collected') loses to them. A third mini-example: the compliance report. 'Regulators require evidence that access reviews happen quarterly.' The report includes the review dates, the reviewers, the findings, and the remediation - the who, when, what, and so-what. The exam tests whether you know that compliance reporting is evidence of process, not narrative. Common mistake: writing metrics without baselines. 'MTTR is 2 hours' means nothing; 'MTTR fell from 8 hours to 2 hours after the playbook update' proves improvement. Always pair the number with its before."""
EXP[('IL29-M34',5)] = """A second worked example - the weekly rhythm in practice. Monday's 20-minute log set surfaces a weakness: you keep misclassifying beaconing as normal traffic. Tuesday through Thursday, every triage set includes two beaconing scenarios until the pattern is reflex. Friday's assessment: a fresh 15-question set where beaconing appears three times - you nail all three, and the crosswalk table gets a 'validated' mark for the detection topic. That is the plan working as designed: the scorecard finds the weakness, the reps fix it, the assessment proves it. A common failure mode is studying without the Friday assessment - reps without measurement drift into comfortable repetition of what you already know. The assessment is the forcing function; never skip it. And a final gate nuance: when you pass twice consecutively, change nothing about your routine for the last week before the exam except tapering volume. Athletes taper; analysts should too - the goal on exam day is sharp recall, not exhausted cramming."""

# ---------------- M35 expansions ----------------
EXP[('IL29-M35',0)] = """One more CAT mechanic to internalize: because the exam adapts, early questions matter disproportionately - they set the difficulty trajectory. A confident, careful first twenty questions earns you harder questions, and harder questions are worth more in the adaptive scoring. The practical rule: slow down at the start. Read every stem twice, eliminate the technician's answer first, then choose the manager's answer. Candidates who rush the opening and then settle in have already told the algorithm they are weaker than they are. Second, the 'cannot go back' rule changes guessing strategy: on a linear exam you skip and return, but on CAT each answer is final - so apply the mindset rules decisively, commit, and move on. Lingering on a CAT question does not buy you a better answer; it buys you time pressure on the questions the algorithm still needs to measure you."""
EXP[('IL29-M35',1)] = """A third worked example - the 'two right answers' drill. Scenario: 'A developer asks for production database access to debug a customer issue.' Answer A: 'Grant read-only access for today.' Answer B: 'Deny and require the ticket to go through the data-access approval process with masked data.' The technician picks A (fast, helpful, read-only feels safe). The CISSP answer is B: governance before convenience - the approval process exists to weigh the risk, and production data in debugging is an asset-security question, not a favor. The exam constructs these pairs constantly: the quick helpful answer vs. the process answer. Train the reflex: when you see the pair, the process answer wins unless human safety is at stake - in which case the safety answer beats everything. A second mindset anchor: 'the business decides.' CISSP never lets the technician make the business's risk decision. Your job in every scenario is to frame the risk, present options with costs, and let leadership choose - then execute faithfully. Candidates who 'decide for the business' fail even when their technical judgment was sound."""
EXP[('IL29-M35',2)] = """A third worked example - the legal/regulatory discriminator. Scenario: 'A company operating in the EU suffers a breach of customer data.' The Domain 1 concepts in play: breach-notification duties under GDPR (the exam expects you to know that notification obligations exist and are time-bound, without demanding exact hour counts from memory), the need for legal counsel before public statements, and evidence preservation for potential investigation. The CISSP-correct sequence: contain, engage legal, preserve evidence, notify per the applicable regulation's process. The trap answers are 'notify customers immediately on social media' (legal reviews first) and 'delete the attacker artifacts to clean up' (destroys evidence). A second Domain 2 drill: data retention. 'The legal team requires financial records kept seven years; the privacy team wants minimal retention.' The resolution: the data owner balances the requirements in the retention policy - legal hold overrides deletion schedules, and the policy documents the decision. The exam loves these tension scenarios; the answer is always the documented policy decision, not either extreme."""
EXP[('IL29-M35',3)] = """A third worked example - crypto concepts at the CISSP level. Scenario: 'Two parties need to exchange sensitive design documents with confidentiality, integrity, and non-repudiation.' The CISSP answer: encrypt with the recipient's public key (confidentiality - only the private-key holder reads it), sign with the sender's private key (non-repudiation and authenticity), and the recipient verifies the signature with the sender's public key and hashes the document (integrity). The exam tests whether you can assemble the primitives into a solution, not whether you can do the math. The classic trap: 'encrypt with your private key for confidentiality' - wrong, because anyone with your public key can decrypt it; private-key operations prove identity (signing), public-key operations protect confidentiality. A second network-security discriminator: the exam separates secure protocols from their insecure ancestors relentlessly - SSH replaces Telnet, HTTPS replaces HTTP, SFTP/FTPS replace FTP. When a scenario shows Telnet or FTP in use, the finding is the protocol, and the fix is its secure replacement."""
EXP[('IL29-M35',4)] = """A third worked example - the SDLC gate question. Scenario: 'A company wants to stop injection flaws from reaching production.' The Domain 8 answer is a set of SDLC gates: secure-coding standards, automated static analysis in the pipeline, mandatory peer code review for data-access code, and dynamic testing in staging - with a policy that blocks release when gates fail. Patching production after each incident is the wrong answer (treats symptoms); the gates treat the disease. The exam's discriminator: any answer choice that fixes the process beats any choice that fixes the instance. A second Domain 6 drill: 'The audit committee wants assurance that the new access-control system works as designed.' The answer is a controls assessment against the requirements - not a pen test (which would test whether an attacker can bypass it, a different question). The exam separates 'does it work as designed' (assessment/audit) from 'can it be broken' (pen test) from 'what weaknesses exist' (vuln scan) with near-religious consistency. Memorize the three questions each answers and you will not miss these."""
EXP[('IL29-M35',5)] = """A second worked example - the decision log catching a drift. Midway through Phase 4 you notice your log shows five entries where you picked the technical answer over the management one - all in Domain 7 scenarios about 'restoring service fast.' The pattern reveals your bias: under operations pressure you revert to technician. The fix is a personal rule added to the log's front page: 'In Domain 7, the plan is the answer - invoke the procedure before improvising.' Two weeks later the same scenario type appears and you pick the management answer without hesitation. That is the decision log working as a bias-correction instrument, not just a notebook. A final exam-week protocol: stop new material five days out; run only timed scenario sets and decision-log reviews. Sleep eight hours the last three nights - CAT punishes fatigue more than linear exams do, because every question demands judgment, and judgment is the first casualty of tiredness."""

# ---------------- M36 expansions ----------------
EXP[('IL29-M36',0)] = """A second margin scenario shows why the index must be fast. Suppose 30 questions require lookups. At 60 seconds each, lookups cost 30 minutes - leaving 210 minutes for 76 questions, nearly 3 minutes each: comfortable. At 3 minutes per lookup, the same 30 lookups cost 90 minutes - leaving 150 minutes for 76 questions, under 2 minutes each: panic. The difference between a 60-second index and a 3-minute rummage is the entire exam. Build the index for speed: one term, one location, no ambiguity - and drill it until your hand moves before your brain finishes reading the question. A final open-book trap: the 'just in case' library. Candidates bring five references 'in case' and waste the first twenty minutes deciding which book holds the answer. One primary reference, indexed and tabbed, beats a library every time. If a second reference earns its place, it is for exactly one topic area - and its tabs say so."""
EXP[('IL29-M36',1)] = """A third worked example - the layer-failure audit in action. 'The company relies on a single firewall for network security, with no host firewalls, no EDR, and flat internal networking.' The audit finding: four missing layers - host, detection, segmentation, and monitoring. The remediation priority: EDR on endpoints first (detects what the firewall misses), then host firewalls and segmentation (contains what EDR misses), then centralized logging (sees what everything missed). The exam's discriminator: the best next control is the one that covers the largest uncovered attack surface, not the one that strengthens an existing layer. A second nuance: defense in depth includes the human and process layers - the phishing-resistant culture from awareness training and the tested incident-response plan are layers with the same status as the firewall. When a scenario asks what 'completes' a defense-in-depth design, look for the missing people-or-process answer; the exam plants it as the correct choice among shiny technical options."""
EXP[('IL29-M36',2)] = """A third worked example - the accountability drill. 'Three administrators share the root password on a Linux server; a destructive command runs at 3 AM and nobody admits it.' The failed A is accountability - shared credentials make actions untraceable. The fix has three parts: individual sudo accounts (identification + authentication per person), sudoers restricting who may run what (authorization), and centralized logging of every sudo invocation (accountability). The exam tests this as a before/after: the before violates accountability, the after restores it. Common trap: answering with 'stronger passwords.' The password was not the problem - the sharing was. When a stem shows shared accounts, the finding is always accountability, and the fix is always individual identities plus logging. A second trap: MFA on shared accounts. MFA strengthens authentication but does nothing for accountability - five people with the same MFA-enrolled account are still untraceable. Fix the identity model first."""
EXP[('IL29-M36',3)] = """A third worked example - key management as the exam's crypto trap. 'A company encrypts its backups with AES but stores the key in a plaintext file next to the backups.' The finding: the crypto is theater - anyone who steals the backups steals the key. The fix is key management: generate keys securely, store them separately (a key-management system or HSM), rotate on schedule and on personnel changes, and destroy keys when data is retired (cryptographic erasure). The exam's rule: every crypto scenario is really a key-management scenario. The algorithm choice is rarely the finding; the key handling almost always is. A second discriminator: digital signatures vs. HMAC. Signatures use asymmetric keys and provide non-repudiation (the signer cannot deny signing); HMAC uses a shared secret and proves integrity and authenticity between parties who share it, but not non-repudiation. When the stem needs 'prove who sent it to a third party,' the answer is signatures."""
EXP[('IL29-M36',4)] = """A third worked example - the chain-of-custody drill. 'An analyst copies suspicious files to a USB drive for analysis, then hands the drive to a colleague with no documentation.' Two failures: the copy is not a forensic image (hashes undocumented, original altered by the copy process), and the handoff has no custody record (who had it, when, why). The correct procedure: image the original with a write blocker, hash the image, log every handler and transfer, analyze the copy. The exam treats any break in this chain as evidence contamination - and in legal contexts, contaminated evidence is inadmissible. A second trap: the 'quick peek' - 'the analyst opens the malware sample on their workstation to see what it does.' That is detonation, not analysis: it alerts the attacker (if beaconing), contaminates the workstation, and destroys the forensic state. Analysis happens on isolated forensic copies, never on production systems. When a stem shows analysis on the original or on a live workstation, the finding is procedure, and the fix is the forensic workflow."""
EXP[('IL29-M36',5)] = """A third worked example - the GPO-vs-manual discriminator. 'Five hundred workstations need USB mass storage blocked.' The manual answer (visit each machine) is operationally absurd; the GSEC answer is Group Policy - one policy, enforced centrally, auditable, reversible. The exam's scale rule: any control applied to more than a handful of systems must be centrally managed, or it is not a control but a wish. A second Linux drill: 'The web server's document root is world-writable.' The finding: excessive permissions (any local user can alter served content - a defacement and malware-distribution risk). The fix: owner-writable only, group-readable as needed, and the service account given exactly the rights its function requires. The exam pairs permission scenarios with the least-privilege principle constantly: when a stem shows broad permissions, the finding is the permission, and the fix is the minimum necessary. A final cross-platform trap: 'the company encrypts laptops with BitLocker but leaves desktops unencrypted because they never leave the office.' Theft is not the only threat - disposal, repair, and insider access all expose desktop disks. The finding is inconsistent encryption policy; the fix is encryption by data classification, not by device mobility."""
EXP[('IL29-M36',6)] = """A second planning nuance: interleave the tracks. Do not study all knowledge in month one and build the index in month two - the index entries written while learning are sharper than entries reconstructed later. The rule: every study session ends with ten minutes of index maintenance - new terms entered, weak entries improved, cross-references added. A worked example of the habit: Tuesday's crypto session adds 'HMAC,' 'digital signature,' 'key escrow,' and 'cryptographic erasure' with locations and one-line discriminators. By Friday's lookup drill, those four terms resolve in seconds - because you indexed them while the understanding was fresh. A final gate nuance: your two consecutive 80%+ passes must be on FRESH question sets. Passing the same set twice proves memory, not readiness. Build or source at least three full practice sets before you start gating, so the second pass is always a new exam. When the third set also passes, you are not lucky - you are ready."""

# ---------------- M37 expansions ----------------
EXP[('IL29-M37',0)] = """A second worked example - the honest comparison. Google's certificate, as publicly described, is the broader on-ramp: foundations, analyst tools, and workflows for true beginners. IBM's is the analyst-role deepening: closer to SOC tasks and analytical thinking. Neither description claims to produce a senior operator - and that honesty is useful, because it tells you exactly where IL-29 takes over. The crosswalk's job is to draw that handoff line precisely: everything left of the line, validate via the certificate; everything right of it, build via IL-29 labs. A common mistake is choosing a certificate by brand instead of by fit. The fit question is simple: which of your gaps does its public topic map cover, in what sequence, and with what assessments? Answer that from the course pages - not from marketing - and the choice makes itself. And a standing caution for the whole module: certificate pages change. Re-verify the topic map from the public pages the week you build your crosswalk; a table built on last year's syllabus quietly misleads you about this year's gaps."""
EXP[('IL29-M37',1)] = """A third worked example - the foundations trap. Suppose your self-assessment says networking is 'fine,' but the certificate's first assessment includes subnetting and you score 60%. The crosswalk verdict for networking flips from 'validate' to 'study' - and the study is IL-29's networking modules plus timed subnetting drills, not rewatching the certificate's videos. The principle: assessments diagnose, IL-29 treats. The certificate tells you WHERE you are weak with precision; IL-29 gives you the depth to fix it. That division of labor is the entire value proposition of pairing the two. A second nuance: guided labs vs. open labs. Certificate labs are guided - they walk you through the correct procedure, which is perfect for first exposure. IL-29 labs are open - they give you the objective and let you fight through it, which is what builds operator judgment. Do the guided lab first for the pattern, then the open lab for the judgment. In that order, the pair produces both competence and confidence; reversed, the open lab just frustrates."""
EXP[('IL29-M37',2)] = """A third worked example - the report artifact in detail. Take a practice incident: a phishing email, a payload, a contained host. The executive summary (one page): what happened, business impact, what was done, what it costs to prevent recurrence, residual risk. The technical report: full timeline with timestamps, IOCs, the containment and eradication steps, verification results. The timeline graphic: the incident as a visual sequence for the post-incident review. Three documents, one incident, three audiences - and producing all three from one scenario is the single best preparation for both the certificate's communication content and the panel's 'how do you communicate' questions. A common mistake is writing the technical report first and 'summarizing' it for executives. That produces a summary full of technical residue. Write the executive version first, from the business impact outward - it forces you to decide what actually mattered. Then write the technical version with everything the engineers need. Different documents, different thinking, both required."""
EXP[('IL29-M37',3)] = """A third worked example - the table as a living document. Month one: 40 rows, 12 marked 'study.' Month two: 8 of the 12 flip to 'validated' as assessments pass; 2 new gaps appear (topics you did not know you did not know - the table's hidden gift). Month three: the table is 90% validated, and the remaining rows are your genuine frontier - the topics worth deep IL-29 lab investment. The table did not just prevent duplication; it mapped your ignorance precisely, which is the most valuable study artifact there is. A maintenance rule: revisit the table monthly. Certificate pages update, IL-29 grows, and your knowledge changes - a stale crosswalk quietly rots into false confidence. Fifteen minutes a month keeps it honest. A second discipline: the 'skip' verdict needs justification. Marking a topic 'skip' because it is boring is how gaps survive; marking it 'skip' with a written reason ('out of scope for the Oct 13 panel; revisit Q1') is a decision. The table records decisions, not moods."""
EXP[('IL29-M37',4)] = """A third worked example - the taper week. Week 8 is not for new topics: it is full timed sets, panel sims from M39/M40, and artifact polish. Monday: timed 40-question set, score by domain. Tuesday: M39 technical bank aloud, rubric-scored. Wednesday: M40 STAR bank aloud, timed. Thursday: weakest-domain drills only. Friday: rest or light review - the athlete's taper. The science is simple: retrieval practice under mild time pressure consolidates skill; cramming new material the night before degrades it. A second rhythm nuance: protect the morning. If your sharpest hours are 6-9 AM, that is when the hard reps go - triage sets, timed banks, new labs. Afternoons get review, index maintenance, and artifact writing. Ninety focused morning minutes beat three distracted evening hours; schedule like it. A final integration point: the weekly review is also when you update the crosswalk table and the portfolio. Learning, mapping, and proving in one weekly loop - that is the integrated plan running as designed, and it is sustainable for exactly as long as the Oct 13 deadline requires."""
EXP[('IL29-M37',5)] = """A second worked example - the artifact review ritual. Monthly, read your three oldest artifacts as if you were the panel. The triage write-up from month one: would it impress you now? If yes, your standards held. If it looks thin, that is growth - annotate what you would add today and let the annotation become a new drill. The portfolio is not a trophy case; it is a training log that happens to be public. A final caution about publishing: sanitize everything. Practice incidents use invented hostnames and IOCs - never real employer data, never real customer information. The panel respects operational security in your portfolio; a write-up leaking real internal details fails the interview it was meant to win. Build the habit now: every artifact gets a sanitization pass before it goes anywhere public. Proof of work, zero leakage - that is the professional standard."""

# ---------------- M38 expansions ----------------
EXP[('IL29-M38',0)] = """A second reading of the topic map as career positioning. Foundational plus defensive plus offensive is the full-stack security education in one program - the graduate can discuss governance with the CISO, triage with the SOC, and attack chains with the red team. That breadth is precisely what makes the program attractive to career-switchers and upskillers: it produces conversational fluency across the whole discipline, not just one silo. Your IL-29 preparation should mirror that breadth deliberately - resist the temptation to go deep on only your favorite domain. The program will test the full map; arrive with no dark corners. A practical method: the topic-map checklist. List every publicly described element, rate your confidence 1-5, and attack the 1s and 2s first. Confidence ratings are honest only if you test them - the self-tests in the following notes are the instrument. And a final note on the numbers: 10-20 hours a week for 24 weeks is 240-480 total hours. That is a part-time job. Treat the application decision with the gravity of accepting a job - because in time commitment, that is what it is."""
EXP[('IL29-M38',1)] = """A third self-test prompt set, because foundations have more corners than learners expect. Explain DNS resolution from keystroke to IP address. Explain what happens when you type a URL and press enter - the full chain: DNS, TCP handshake, TLS negotiation, HTTP request. Explain the difference between a hub, a switch, and a router in one sentence each. Explain symmetric vs. asymmetric crypto with the TLS handshake as the example. Four prompts, each answerable in 90 seconds by someone whose foundations are solid - and each exposing a specific gap when they are not. The 'type a URL' question is the classic: it touches DNS, TCP, TLS, HTTP, and rendering, and a fluent answer proves integrated understanding rather than isolated facts. If any prompt stalls you, the remediation is the same IL-29 loop: the relevant module, the lab, the teach-back. A final foundations trap: confusing familiarity with fluency. You have SEEN the TCP handshake a dozen times; can you DRAW it from memory, with sequence numbers, right now? Fluency is the standard - the program assumes it on day one."""
EXP[('IL29-M38',2)] = """A third defensive drill - the detection-engineering rep. Write a detection rule for credential dumping: what log source (Windows Security log, specific event IDs), what pattern (LSASS access from an unusual process), what threshold (any single occurrence - this is never benign), what response (isolate, alert as critical). Then tune it: the backup agent touches LSASS legitimately every night - add the exclusion with a comment explaining why, and document the residual risk. That write-tune-document cycle IS defensive operations at the practitioner level, and doing it three times for three techniques builds more readiness than reading about detection for a week. A second defensive nuance: the program's defensive arc will assume you understand the kill chain as the attacker's complement to your IR lifecycle. Practice the pairing: for each IR phase, name the attacker phase it counters - detection counters execution, containment counters lateral movement, eradication counters persistence. The pairing turns two memorized lists into one understood conflict."""
EXP[('IL29-M38',3)] = """A third offensive exercise - the purple-team table. Take one ATT&CK technique, e.g., scheduled-task persistence. Red side: how would you establish it, and what would it look like in the logs? Blue side: what detection covers it, and what is the false-positive rate? Then the purple question: what did the exercise teach both sides? Running that table for five techniques builds the offensive-defensive integration the program's arc is designed to produce - and it is directly the mindset the ThreatLocker panel rewards: the operator who thinks like the attacker to defend like a professional. A common mistake in offensive prep is collecting technique names without the detection pairing. A technique you cannot detect is trivia; a technique paired with its detection is capability. For every offensive concept you study, finish with the two sentences: 'I would detect this by...' and 'I would stop this by...' Those sentences are the entire return on investment of the offensive phase."""
EXP[('IL29-M38',4)] = """A third capstone criterion: the demo. A capstone that can be demonstrated - the rules firing on test traffic, the triage runbook executed live, the walkthrough video - beats a capstone that can only be described. Design for demoability from the draft stage: what will you SHOW, not just what will you write? The demonstration is also your panel asset: 'here is my capstone walkthrough' is a stronger Oct 13 artifact than any certificate. A second drafting discipline: the one-page test. If your capstone proposal cannot be understood from one page - problem, scope, program elements, done-criterion, demo - it is not ready. Complexity that cannot be summarized is usually confusion, not depth. Rewrite until the page is clear; the clarity will survive into the execution. And a final topic filter: choose the capstone you will still care about in month five. Twenty-four weeks is long enough for enthusiasm to die - pick the problem that genuinely bothers you, because bothered is sustainable and bored is not."""
EXP[('IL29-M38',5)] = """A final checklist discipline: the pre-application review. One week before applying, do a full mock week at the program's pace - 15 hours of structured study across the topic map, timed and logged. If the week feels sustainable, your time audit was honest. If it breaks you, the audit lied - and better to learn that from a mock week than from week three of a $7,750 program. The mock week is the last and most honest readiness instrument in this module: it tests the plan against reality before reality starts charging tuition."""

# ---------------- M39 expansions ----------------
EXP[('IL29-M39',3)] = """One more panel nuance: when asked about elevation, always mention the audit trail - every just-in-time elevation is logged with the application, the time, and the policy that granted it. Panels love the accountability angle: least privilege plus full visibility is the complete answer. And connect it to the hiring logic: the IT Infrastructure Specialist who can explain WHY the control exists (malware inherits privilege) and HOW it preserves workflow (the app elevates, the user does not) is demonstrating exactly the operator-plus-communicator blend the role demands."""

# ---------------- M40 expansions ----------------
EXP[('IL29-M40',0)] = """A third worked example - the 90-second budget as a rehearsal tool. Record yourself answering one scaffold aloud, then transcribe it and count the words per beat. Typical first attempts: 100 words of Situation, 20 of Task, 60 of Action, 10 of Result - the exact inversion of the ideal. The transcription does not lie, and one round of it fixes the shape permanently: cut the Situation to the incident, expand the Action to your steps, and give the Result its numbers. A second timing trick: the headline. Open every answer with a one-sentence headline ('I contained a three-host incident in 20 minutes') before the STAR detail. The headline buys you the panel's attention for the remaining 75 seconds - and if nerves ever cut an answer short, the headline alone still landed the point. Practice headlines separately: one sentence per story, specific and quantified, until they are automatic."""
EXP[('IL29-M40',1)] = """A third delivery scenario - the interrupted answer. Midway through your Action beat, a panelist interjects: 'Wait - why did you isolate before blocking the C2?' The wrong response is fluster and restart. The right response: answer the interjection directly ('Isolation stops lateral movement in seconds; the firewall block took two minutes to propagate - so isolation bought the containment window'), then bridge back ('...which is why the next step was the C2 block you asked about'). Interruptions are engagement, not attacks - the panelist is interested enough to dig. Welcome the dig: it lets you show the reasoning behind the steps, which is what senior interviewers score highest. A rehearsal method: have a partner interrupt every practice answer with one 'why' question. After a week of it, real interruptions feel like the practice - which is the entire point of rehearsal."""
EXP[('IL29-M40',2)] = """A fourth support prompt for the bank - Prompt 3b: 'How do you explain a technical problem to a non-technical person?' The Cyber Hero answer: analogies anchored in the user's world, no jargon, check for understanding, and the fix framed as what THEY need to do (one clear action). Scaffold: S-[a real explanation you gave], T-[what the user needed to understand or do], A-[your explanation approach: the analogy, the single action item, the confirmation], R-[the user succeeded / the ticket closed cleanly]. This prompt appears constantly in support-adjacent roles because it tests the rarest combination: deep technical understanding expressed simply. The trap is the expert's curse - explaining at YOUR level instead of theirs. The fix is the teach-back: 'just to make sure I explained it clearly, could you tell me what you will do next?' - which verifies understanding while respecting the user. Practice one teach-back line until it sounds natural, not scripted."""
EXP[('IL29-M40',3)] = """A fourth pressure prompt - Prompt 6b: 'Tell me about a time you disagreed with a decision during an incident.' The panel tests whether you can dissent productively under load: state the disagreement crisply, propose the alternative with its tradeoff, accept the call once made, and execute fully. Scaffold: S-[the real incident and the decision], T-[your role and what you saw differently], A-[how you raised it: the data, the alternative, the acceptance], R-[the outcome and what the team learned about dissent]. The scoring: loyalty to the decision after it is made matters as much as the courage to raise the concern. 'I argued, lost, then slow-walked the plan' fails catastrophically; 'I argued with data, the lead chose otherwise, I executed fully, and we debriefed after' passes cleanly. Panels ask this because incident dissent handled badly destroys teams - show them you strengthen the team instead."""
EXP[('IL29-M40',4)] = """A third integrity prompt - Prompt 8b: 'Tell me about a time you received critical feedback on your technical work.' Distinct from Prompt 8 (hard to hear, personal): this one is about the work product - a code review, a config, an analysis that was wrong. Scaffold: S-[the real feedback: what was wrong], T-[what you were responsible for], A-[how you received it (no defensiveness), verified the critique, fixed the work, changed your review process], R-[the improved output and the process change that stuck]. The scoring: defensiveness is the failure mode. 'My first reaction was to explain why I was right; then I tested their claim, they were right, and I fixed it' is a perfect answer - it shows the honest internal reaction AND the professional override. Panels know everyone feels defensive; they are checking whether professionalism wins. A rehearsal note: this story must be about the WORK, not the person - keep every sentence on the artifact and the process, never on personalities."""
EXP[('IL29-M40',5)] = """A fourth growth prompt - Prompt 11b: 'What is the most useful thing you have learned in the last six months?' The panel tests learning velocity and reflection. Your honest answer draws on this course: the specific operator skill (e.g., Detect triage discipline, ringfencing tradeoff reasoning), why it mattered, and how you learned it (the method from Prompt 9). Scaffold: S-[where you started], T-[what you needed], A-[the learning reps], R-[the capability now, with evidence]. The trap: naming something vague ('I learned a lot about security'). The fix: one specific skill, the method, the proof. Specificity signals real learning; vagueness signals real forgetting. Prepare this answer as the capstone of your growth narrative - it is the story of the last six months told as trajectory, and trajectory is what gets offers."""
EXP[('IL29-M40',6)] = """A final logistics layer: the 24 hours before. Confirm the route and parking the night before - a lost candidate arrives flustered, and flustered fails STAR. Prepare your bag: ID, printed resume copies, a notebook, water. Eat a real breakfast; low blood sugar destroys 90-second discipline. And set the mental frame on the drive: you are not begging for a job, you are showing a panel how you work - the rehearsal already proved you can do it. That frame shift from supplicant to demonstrator changes posture, voice, and answers. Walk in as the operator you have spent weeks becoming, not the applicant hoping to become one. The work is done. October 13 is the demonstration."""

